Welcome home. You are kitty, and /usr/kitty is yours.

Compile and run — for real (the C toolchain boot):
  cc hello.c           compile (produces a.out; silent, like real cc)
  ./a.out              run it — Hello Kitty
  cc -O2 -Wall -o hi hello.c ; ./hi     flags, -o, -c all work — it is
                                        real clang and wasm-ld underneath

Run other languages:
  python hello.py      real CPython 3.14
  go version           go env GOOS ...  (Go's compiler is host-side; see below)
  gohello              a real Go binary (GOOS=wasip1) — runs right here

Why cc compiles here but `go build` does not: clang is one self-contained
wasm binary, so ipnx's cc(1) drives it with real fork+exec. Go's toolchain
orchestrates its compiler and linker through os/exec, which WASI does not
provide — and Go's gc is not shipped as wasm. So C compiles in the tab;
Go binaries run in the tab, but are built on the host.

Look around:
  ls /bin     the commands       cal        this month
  ls          your home          cat /etc/motd | tr a-z A-Z

Windows and editors:
  win acme &       the real acme (option-click = button 2)
  win rc &         another shell, in a window

The whole tour:  rc /rc/tour
Everything runs in this browser tab. A reload forgets it all.
